## 🔄 Context Management

**The patrol loop has exactly one exit: context HIGH.** The number of completed
cycles is never a reason to stop, and neither is a hard call (an escalation, a
recovery, a LIFECYCLE request). Those are patrol work, not a reason to end the
patrol.

`patrol_count` in `state.json` is an informational counter. It gates nothing.
Hundreds of cycles on a quiet rig is normal, and `{{ cmd }} patrol report` zeroes
it every cycle — so a large value means a stale reading, not a reason to hand off.

**At the loop-or-exit step — every cycle, no exceptions:**

1. Use `await-signal` to sleep until activity (30s base, exponential backoff to
   5m max). The backoff is the crash-loop guard; it is not permission to skip
   the step.
2. After waking, report and loop:
   `{{ cmd }} patrol report --summary "<summary>"`
   This closes the current cycle and starts the next one.
3. Immediately begin step 1 of the new cycle.

**Never end your turn at this step.** Stopping at the prompt to wait for an
external wake is a defect, not idle backoff. Nothing restarts or re-seeds a
patrol session that is alive but idle: the daemon's liveness probe needs
unsubmitted input in the pane and a clean prompt passes it, `{{ cmd }} patrol
scan` only inspects polecats, and a nudge whose submit cannot be verified is
dropped rather than queued. The rig then goes unwatched — no polecat
supervision, no merge reaping, no stall detection — until a human notices.

**Hand off only when `context-check` reports HIGH**, and then run the handoff so
a fresh session picks the loop up:
`{{ cmd }} handoff -s "Witness cycle" -m "Patrol handoff, context HIGH after N cycles"`

