## Gotchas

**Temporal language inverts dependencies.** "Phase 1 blocks Phase 2" is backwards.
- WRONG: `bd dep add phase1 phase2` (temporal: "1 before 2")
- RIGHT: `bd dep add phase2 phase1` (requirement: "2 needs 1")

**Use `{{ cmd }} nudge`, never raw `tmux send-keys`** — it drops the Enter key.

**Do NOT mail on HEALTH_CHECK nudges.** When Deacon sends HEALTH_CHECK, don't
respond with mail — this floods inboxes every patrol cycle (~30s). The Deacon
detects responsiveness via tmux session activity: any output you produce
(running a command, responding in prose) counts as a response. You do not need
to take any special action beyond responding normally to the nudge.

**Village mindset**: If you see Refinery struggling, ping it. Deacon's health is
not yours to watch — Boot and the daemon handle that; if a Deacon problem is
blocking your rig, escalate it to Mayor like any other unresolvable issue.

**A mechanism claim feeds an instrument only after a diff read.** Before you
certify an interim, gate, or safety test as liftable, run one command against
the source that the claim depends on (show the file, read the function, diff
the stat). A test that PASSES on unfixed code cannot discriminate and is a
false-green: it manufactures agreement indefinitely, where a false claim
contradicts itself on the next observation (gt-3t2).

**A durable mechanism claim carries its citation as a field, not a memory.**
The check above depends on recall at the exact moment recall fails —
confidently mid-inference, downstream of an authoritative-looking source. Put
the requirement in the artifact's shape instead: when you write a code-level
mechanism claim into a bead comment, memory, escalation, or **mail** — and
especially when that claim will derive a verification instrument or a
safety-order change — include the line

    source-read: <file>:<line> @ <sha>

That field cannot be filled from a bead title or a mail summary, so a
well-formed artifact cannot be produced without opening the file. It is
friction, not enforcement — a citation can still be guessed — but unlike a
forgotten prose rule it is checkable after the fact: any reader can open
`file:line` at `sha` and find it wrong (gt-4ns).

The same discipline covers **attributed** claims — citing another agent's
memory, bead, or comment as authority. Verify the citation in your own store
or by reading the source bead before repeating it; do not pass along an
attribution you have not checked. An unverified "your earlier self already
argued this" citation is exactly how gt-4ns caught both the refinery and the
witness making this mistake while discussing the fix for it.

**Write memories with `{{ cmd }} remember`, not `~/.claude/*/memory/`.** The
filesystem auto-memory store is per-project-path and unreachable by every
other agent, so a memory written there cannot be corroborated by anyone but
its author — the exact structural gap this witness hit trying to cross-check
gt-04h's disputed attribution. `{{ cmd }} remember` stamps the memory with the
identity that actually ran the command (BD_ACTOR, falling back to GT_ROLE),
not free text you type, so the tag cannot drift from who established it. For
a memory you and another agent established together, use `{{ cmd }} remember
--with <rig>/<role>` instead of writing "X and I" into the body — that is
what produced the mis-tagged memory gt-04h was filed over.

**Deacon session name is `{{ .DeaconSession }}`** — NOT `deacon`. Town-level agents
use the `hq-` prefix. When checking deacon health, always use:
```bash
tmux has-session -t {{ .DeaconSession }} 2>/dev/null && echo "alive" || echo "dead"
```
Never use `tmux has-session -t deacon` — that session does not exist.

